  "content": [
    {
      "id": "introduction",
      "title": "Introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "You already know how to merge&nbsp;<strong>two</strong>&nbsp;sorted lists. K-way merge extends that to&nbsp;<strong>K sorted lists at the same time</strong>.",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "1-the-problem",
      "title": "1. The Problem",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "You have K sorted lists. Merge them into one single sorted list.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406460340-149244091.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406460340-149244091.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406460340-149244091.png",
          "alt": "The Problem"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "This diagram shows how we merge multiple sorted lists step by step using the&nbsp;<strong>smallest available element each time</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "At the start, we take the first elements from all lists (1, 2, 3) and pick the smallest →&nbsp;<strong>1</strong>.&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Then we move forward in that list and bring its next element (4) into consideration.&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Now again compare current heads (2, 3, 4), pick smallest →&nbsp;<strong>2</strong>, and repeat the process.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "At every step, we always choose the&nbsp;<strong>minimum among the current elements of all lists</strong>, add it to the result, and move forward in that list.&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This continues until all elements are used, giving the final merged sorted list:&nbsp;<strong>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9]</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "2-why-not-merge-two-at-a-time",
      "title": "2. Why Not Merge Two at a Time?",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "You could repeatedly merge two lists at a time:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406460340-832129582.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406460340-832129582.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406460340-832129582.png",
          "alt": "Why Not Merge Two at a Time?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Merging two lists again and again is slow because you keep <b>re-processing </b>the same elements multiple times as the list keeps growing.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Using a min heap is better because you always pick the smallest element directly, so each element is used only once&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This works but is <b>inefficient</b>:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Each merge becomes bigger, so it takes more time every step.</span><br><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">With K lists, total work becomes&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">O(n × K²)</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">, which is very slow for large K&nbsp;</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "3-the-heap-approach",
      "title": "3. The Heap Approach",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Instead of merging two at a time, use a&nbsp;<strong>min heap</strong>&nbsp;to always pick the globally smallest element across all lists simultaneously.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The idea:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406460340-778361345.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406460340-778361345.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406460340-778361345.png",
          "alt": "The Heap Approach"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "Push the first element of each list into the min heap",
            "Remove the smallest element → add it to result",
            "Push the next element from the same list",
            "Repeat until heap is empty&nbsp;"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The heap always holds exactly one element from each list that still has elements remaining. Its top is always the current global minimum.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "Step by Step",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Lists:<br>L1:&nbsp;<code>[1, 4, 7]</code><br>L2:&nbsp;<code>[2, 5, 8]</code><br>L3:&nbsp;<code>[3, 6, 9]</code>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Initial:</strong><br>Push first elements → Heap =&nbsp;<code>[1, 2, 3]</code>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong><img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406460341-398819262.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\"></strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Step 1:</strong><br></strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Pop&nbsp;<code>1</code>&nbsp;→ Output:&nbsp;<code>[1]</code><br>Push next from L1 →&nbsp;<code>4</code><br>Heap:&nbsp;<code>[2, 3, 4]</code>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong><img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406460341-463945489.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\"></strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Step 2:</strong><br></strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Pop&nbsp;<code>2</code>&nbsp;→ Output:&nbsp;<code>[1, 2]</code><br>Push next from L2 →&nbsp;<code>5</code><br>Heap:&nbsp;<code>[3, 4, 5]</code>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong><img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406460341-808419093.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\"></strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Step 3:</strong><br></strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Pop&nbsp;<code>3</code>&nbsp;→ Output:&nbsp;<code>[1, 2, 3]</code><br>Push next from L3 →&nbsp;<code>6</code><br>Heap:&nbsp;<code>[4, 5, 6]</code>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong><img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406460341-950920736.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\"></strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Step 4:</strong><br></strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Pop&nbsp;<code>4</code>&nbsp;→ Output:&nbsp;<code>[1, 2, 3, 4]</code><br>Push next from L1 →&nbsp;<code>7</code><br>Heap:&nbsp;<code>[5, 6, 7]</code>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406460851-113403574.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\"><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Continue same process →</span>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Final Output:&nbsp;<code>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9]</code>&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "What We are Doing Here:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Push first element of each list into a min heap.&nbsp;<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Repeatedly remove the smallest element and add it to the result.</span>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Then push the next element from the same list until all elements are processed",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "public List<Integer> mergeKLists(int[][] lists) {\n    List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();\n\n    // min heap stores [value, listIndex, elementIndex]\n    PriorityQueue<int[]> minHeap = new PriorityQueue<>(\n        (a, b) -> a[0] - b[0]    // sort by value\n    );\n\n    // Step 1 — push first element of every list\n    for (int i = 0; i < lists.length; i++) {\n        if (lists[i].length > 0) {\n            minHeap.offer(new int[]{lists[i][0], i, 0});\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Step 2 — keep popping minimum and pushing next\n    while (!minHeap.isEmpty()) {\n        int[] curr = minHeap.poll();\n        int val   = curr[0];\n        int list  = curr[1];\n        int idx   = curr[2];\n\n        result.add(val);    // add to output\n\n        // push next element from same list\n        if (idx + 1 < lists[list].length) {\n            minHeap.offer(new int[]{lists[list][idx + 1], list, idx + 1});\n        }\n    }\n\n    return result;\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Complexity",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Total elements = n × K",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Each element is pushed and popped once → each operation takes O(log K)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Total time =&nbsp;<strong>O(n × K × log K)</strong><br>Space =&nbsp;<strong>O(K)</strong>&nbsp;(heap size at most K)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Compared to naive O(n × K²), heap approach is much faster for large K&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "4-where-this-pattern-appears",
      "title": "4. Where This Pattern Appears",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Merge K sorted arrays → push first element of each</span><br>",
            "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Merge K sorted linked lists → push head of each list</span><br>",
            "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Kth smallest in K sorted lists → stop after K pops</span><br>",
            "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Smallest range covering K lists → track min and max together</span>"
          ]
        },
        {
